"use strict";
// Repository-specific teaching content. No credentials or private key material.
window.shadowLessons = [
{id:"orientation",group:"FOUNDATIONS",title:"Start with the whole system",subtitle:"Six layers. One laptop. A clear place for every change.",lab:"map",body:`
<div class="grid"><div class="card"><span class="label">The goal</span><h3>Explain it, then change it.</h3><p>Follow the lessons in order once. Afterward, search by task: add an app, rotate a token, recover a generation, or debug DNS. Mark a lesson understood only when you can answer its question in your own words.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="label">The boundary</span><h3>Source is not the running system.</h3><p>This guide describes the revised repository. Settings become active only after activation; boot changes may require a reboot. The simulators never change your machine.</p></div></div>
<h2>What the names mean</h2><p><strong>Nix</strong> is a package manager and expression language. <strong>Nixpkgs</strong> is a package collection and library. <strong>NixOS</strong> uses Nix to describe an operating system. <strong>Home Manager</strong> describes your user environment. A <strong>flake</strong> gives the project inputs and named outputs. <strong>devenv</strong> gives each development project its own tools and services.</p>
<p>A laptop has more than one kind of state: configuration source, installed software, running services, personal data, credentials, and firmware. Nix describes much of the first three. It does not turn your documents, databases, or firmware settings into rollback-safe Nix values.</p>
<h2>Your system’s shape</h2><pre><code>flake.nix → shadow/default.nix → modules/nixos/
                              → shadow/home.nix → modules/home/
encrypted-secrets/ → sops-nix → protected files in /run
guide/ → this offline learning interface</code></pre>
<p>Shadow is one Razer Blade 14 with AMD integrated graphics and NVIDIA graphics, Niri and Noctalia on Wayland, a tmpfs root, and LUKS-encrypted Btrfs storage. Hardware facts live in <code>shadow/</code>; reusable capabilities live in modules.</p>
<div class="banner">The important habit: locate the owner of a behavior, edit its source, evaluate, build, test, then activate. Do not begin by editing generated files under /etc or the Nix store.</div>`,sources:[["NixOS manual","https://nixos.org/manual/nixos/stable/"],["nix.dev learning paths","https://nix.dev/"]],quiz:["Where should your personal documents be represented?",["As Nix strings in flake.nix","As persistent data with separate backups","As packages in Nixpkgs"],1,"Nix describes how the system is assembled. Documents remain mutable data and need persistence plus backups."]},
{id:"language",group:"FOUNDATIONS",title:"Read the Nix language",subtitle:"Recognize the small patterns used throughout this repository.",body:`
<p>A Nix file is an expression that evaluates to a value. Common values are strings, numbers, booleans, paths, lists, attribute sets, and functions. An attribute set is a mapping of names to values; it is not a shell script. Nix is lazy: values are evaluated when needed.</p>
<pre><code>{ pkgs, lib, ... }:
let
  tools = [ pkgs.ripgrep pkgs.fd ];
in
{
  home.packages = tools;
  programs.fish.enable = true;
}</code></pre>
<p><code>{ pkgs, lib, ... }:</code> is a function that accepts an attribute set. The module system supplies these arguments. <code>...</code> permits additional arguments. <code>let … in …</code> introduces local names. Lists use spaces between items. Attribute assignments end in semicolons. <code>home.packages</code> is a nested attribute, equivalent to <code>home = { packages = tools; };</code>.</p>
<h2>Patterns you will actually edit</h2><ul><li><code>./file.nix</code> is a path relative to the file containing it.</li><li><code>import ./flatpak-apps.nix</code> evaluates that expression. A module’s <code>imports = [ … ];</code> asks the module system to include modules; this has a different job.</li><li><code>inherit appId;</code> means <code>appId = appId;</code> using the surrounding value.</li><li><code>app.origin or "flathub-verified"</code> supplies a fallback when the attribute is missing. This is not boolean OR.</li><li><code>map (x: …) list</code> transforms a list. <code>lib.mapAttrsToList</code> transforms named entries into a list.</li><li><code>a // b</code> shallowly merges attribute sets with right-hand keys winning. It is not the module system’s recursive option merge.</li><li><code>"${"${username}"}"</code> is interpolation: insert a value into a string.</li></ul>
<details><summary>Why a package name sometimes needs pkgs.</summary><p><code>pkgs</code> is the package set. <code>pkgs.ripgrep</code> selects a package from it. <code>with pkgs; [ ripgrep fd ]</code> brings those names into scope for that expression. Prefer explicit names when scope would be confusing.</p></details>
<details><summary>What purity means here</summary><p>Pure evaluation avoids silently depending on unrelated machine state. Flake inputs and tracked files define the source. This helps reproduce a build, but cannot freeze internet services, external Flatpak updates, mutable databases, or firmware behavior.</p></details>`,sources:[["Nix language tutorial","https://nix.dev/tutorials/nix-language"],["Nix reference manual","https://nix.dev/reference/nix-manual"]],quiz:["What does app.origin or \"flathub\" do?",["Combines two origins","Uses flathub when origin is missing","Runs a shell command"],1,"The 'or' syntax supplies an attribute-selection fallback."]},
{id:"modules",group:"FOUNDATIONS",title:"Modules, options & merging",subtitle:"A file being present is not enough. It must be imported.",body:`
<p>Modules contribute definitions to named options. The module system checks types and combines definitions according to each option’s rules. Package lists normally combine; contradictory ordinary scalar definitions normally produce an error rather than silently choosing the last file.</p>
<p><code>config</code> is the evaluated configuration. <code>pkgs</code> provides packages. <code>lib</code> provides module helpers. <code>options</code> describes the available option declarations. Your <code>inputs</code> and <code>username</code> arguments are passed from the host composition, not magically available in every unrelated Nix project.</p>
<pre><code>imports = [ ./power.nix ./audio.nix ];
services.upower.enable = true;
some.option = lib.mkDefault "fallback";
some.option = lib.mkForce "deliberate override";</code></pre>
<p><code>mkDefault</code> gives a low-priority default. <code>mkForce</code> overrides weaker definitions; use it to resolve a specific ownership conflict, not to hide an unexplained error. <code>mkIf</code> conditionally contributes definitions while allowing the module system to resolve dependencies. Read an option’s type and upstream module before assuming the merge behavior.</p>
<h2>Shadow’s import boundary</h2><p><code>shadow/default.nix</code> imports external NixOS modules and selects system domains. <code>shadow/home.nix</code> selects Home Manager domains. A system module should not reach across and import user modules. For example, installing a desktop integration service belongs to NixOS; your browser settings belong to Home Manager.</p>
<p>A <code>default.nix</code> inside a domain is an explicit aggregator. Files omitted from its import list do nothing. Local AI currently illustrates this: its module exists, but its import is parked, so the system does not automatically build or start it.</p>
<div class="banner">To inspect the effective value: <code>nix eval /saved/nixos-config#nixosConfigurations.shadow.config.networking.firewall.enable</code>. Source comments explain intent; evaluated values establish what the module graph actually produces.</div>`,sources:[["Nix module-system tutorial","https://nix.dev/tutorials/module-system/"],["NixOS option search","https://search.nixos.org/options"]],quiz:["You create a new service module. What makes it part of Shadow?",["Its filename","Including it through the host’s import graph","Restarting your editor"],1,"A module contributes only when the configuration imports it."]},
{id:"flakes",group:"FOUNDATIONS",title:"Flakes, locks & trust",subtitle:"Where software comes from, and what an update really changes.",body:`
<p><code>flake.nix</code> describes inputs and outputs. <code>flake.lock</code> records resolved revisions and hashes. The unstable channel URL is a moving source, but the lock records the revision you actually use. Updating inputs changes that pin; it does not immediately change the running system.</p>
<p>The host output is <code>nixosConfigurations.shadow</code>. Its <code>config.system.build.toplevel</code> is the complete system derivation. The formatter is exposed separately, and the Gitleaks source scan is a check. These are named outputs selected with the <code>#</code> part of a flake reference.</p>
<pre><code>delta = {
  url = "github:zed-industries/delta-nix";
  inputs.nixpkgs.follows = "nixpkgs";
};</code></pre>
<p><code>follows</code> aligns Delta’s Nixpkgs with the system’s packages and graphics stack. It avoids an unnecessary independent package-set pin. It is appropriate where upstream supports it; do not apply it blindly to every input. Some projects require their own compatible dependency revisions.</p>
<h2>Three different kinds of trust</h2><ul><li>A source hash establishes identity of fetched content. It does not prove that code is harmless.</li><li>A cache public key verifies signatures on substituted build results. Private signing keys belong to the cache operator.</li><li>Nix <code>trusted-users</code> grants powerful daemon permissions. Shadow trusts root, not the wheel group. You do not need root-equivalent Nix trust just to use a declared binary cache.</li></ul>
<p>Git-backed flakes exclude untracked new files. Use <code>git add</code> for new module files before evaluation; staging is not committing. Read the lock diff after an update. Delta updates through its official input, and its packaged updater is disabled by upstream.</p>`,sources:[["Flakes explained","https://nix.dev/concepts/flakes"],["Official Delta flake","https://github.com/zed-industries/delta-nix"]],quiz:["Does updating flake.lock switch your operating system?",["Yes, immediately","No; build and activation are separate","Only if the file is staged"],1,"Updating records new dependency revisions. The running system changes through activation."]},
{id:"lifecycle",group:"DAILY WORK",title:"Check → build → test → switch",subtitle:"Know exactly which command can change the running machine.",lab:"lifecycle",body:`
<p><strong>Evaluation</strong> computes the configuration and derivations. <strong>Building</strong> realizes the required store paths, using caches where possible. <strong>Activation</strong> updates system links, service definitions, users, and managed files. <strong>Booting</strong> loads the selected kernel/initrd and starts the generation from cold state.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Action</th><th>Changes running system?</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td><code>sys-check</code></td><td>No</td><td>Evaluate and run declared checks.</td></tr><tr><td><code>sys-build</code></td><td>No</td><td>Build the system with nh.</td></tr><tr><td><code>sys-test</code></td><td>Yes, temporarily</td><td>Activate without selecting it as the default boot generation.</td></tr><tr><td><code>sys-switch</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Activate and select the generation for future boots.</td></tr><tr><td><code>sys-update</code></td><td>No</td><td>Update flake inputs only.</td></tr></table></div>
<p>For kernel or graphics-driver changes, a temporary test cannot fully validate the boot path. Review, build, arrange recovery, select the generation for boot, then verify after reboot. A service restart can disrupt ongoing work even if the change is reversible.</p>
<pre><code>cd /saved/nixos-config
git diff
nix fmt
git diff --check
nix flake check
nix build .#nixosConfigurations.shadow.config.system.build.toplevel --no-link
sudo nh os test /saved/nixos-config</code></pre>
<p>After temporary activation, check the affected behavior: fresh login for authentication changes, IPv4/IPv6 DNS for networking, actual app launch and data access for sandbox changes. Keep an authenticated terminal and an older boot generation available. Switch only once the result is understood.</p>
<details><summary>What rollback does not restore</summary><p>A previous generation restores package/configuration references. It does not rewind database migrations, documents, removed files, tokens revoked at a provider, Flatpak app data, or firmware enrollment. A configuration rollback is different from a data restore.</p></details>`,sources:[["NixOS changing configuration","https://nixos.org/manual/nixos/stable/#sec-changing-config"],["nh project","https://github.com/nix-community/nh"]],quiz:["Which action checks your system without activating it?",["sys-switch","sys-test","sys-build"],2,"Build realizes the system. Test and Switch both activate it."]},
{id:"ownership",group:"DAILY WORK",title:"Where to add something",subtitle:"Choose the owner before choosing the file.",lab:"owner",body:`
<p>Start with the behavior you want. A device driver or system service belongs to NixOS. A configured personal application belongs to Home Manager. A compiler version for one project belongs to devenv. An ordinary sandboxed desktop app usually fits the Flatpak catalog.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Change</th><th>Place</th></tr><tr><td>Flatpak app + exceptions</td><td><code>modules/nixos/services/flatpak-apps.nix</code></td></tr><tr><td>Configured native application</td><td><code>modules/home/programs/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Plain GUI / CLI / shared dev tool</td><td><code>modules/home/packages/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Launcher for local AppImage</td><td><code>modules/home/desktop/local-apps.nix</code></td></tr><tr><td>File type default</td><td><code>modules/home/desktop/xdg.nix</code></td></tr><tr><td>System service</td><td>Relevant <code>modules/nixos/</code> domain</td></tr><tr><td>Machine-specific fact</td><td><code>shadow/</code></td></tr></table></div>
<p>Configured modules install their own applications when appropriate. Do not repeat Delta, Brave, or KeePassXC in a plain package list. Delta now comes from the official input with its own desktop entry; the old custom wrapper is removed.</p>
<p>The Flatpak catalog is intentionally ordinary Nix data, not a custom module framework. The service adapter derives installation and overrides. Preservation derives data directories from the same entries. Native applications still need their own persistence declarations because there is no reliable universal rule for where every native app writes.</p>
<h2>Check for duplicates</h2><pre><code>rg 'application-name|application.id' modules shadow flake.nix</code></pre><p>Occurrences can have legitimate roles: installation, settings, MIME defaults, and persistence. The aim is one owner per role, not one occurrence of every name. Removing an app’s install entry is separate from deleting its data.</p>`,sources:[["Home Manager manual","https://nix-community.github.io/home-manager/"],["Flatpak application IDs","https://docs.flatpak.org/en/latest/conventions.html"]],quiz:["Where should a particular project’s Node.js version go?",["Its devenv configuration","The laptop’s global package list","The firewall module"],0,"Project-local toolchains prevent unrelated projects from sharing an accidental global version."]},
{id:"flatpak",group:"DAILY WORK",title:"Add & manage a Flatpak",subtitle:"One catalog entry, three derived behaviors.",lab:"app",body:`
<p>The catalog’s keys are application IDs. An empty entry uses the default verified Flathub remote and retains its data under <code>~/.var/app/&lt;id&gt;</code>. <code>origin</code> chooses a different remote. <code>overrides</code> declares permissions and environment changes. <code>persist = false;</code> is used for theme extensions with no application state.</p>
<pre><code>"org.example.App" = {
  origin = "flathub";
  overrides.Context.filesystems = [ "xdg-download:ro" ];
};</code></pre><p>This is a shape example, not an installable app recommendation. Look up the actual ID, publisher, supported branch, and required permissions before adding it. Verified publishing identifies the publisher; it does not guarantee harmless code or a narrow sandbox.</p>
<h2>After editing</h2><ol><li>Review the app’s upstream permissions and expected workflows.</li><li>Format and evaluate the repository.</li><li>Build, then activate when ready.</li><li>Confirm installation and launch it.</li><li>Check file selection, downloads, audio, camera, screen sharing, and persistence after reboot where relevant.</li></ol>
<pre><code>flatpak list --app --columns=application,origin
flatpak info --show-permissions org.example.App
flatpak override --system --show org.example.App
flatpak override --user --show org.example.App</code></pre>
<p>System and user installations are distinct. This repository owns the system installation, including managed override files. Manual system installs can be removed by reconciliation; manual user installs do not automatically gain declared persistence. A user override may take precedence over system policy and must be inspected when behavior differs.</p>
<p><code>strictFilesystems = true;</code> opts into resetting inherited filesystem grants before applying read-only GTK theme access and this entry’s explicit paths. It is enabled for EarTag, Upscaler, Cine, and Nocturne. Other apps retain their existing narrower manifest permissions until their workflows are reviewed.</p><p>The daily Flatpak timer updates apps independently of <code>flake.lock</code>. NixOS generation rollback does not restore a frozen Flatpak application/data snapshot. Removing the catalog entry stops managing its persistence, but old backing data under <code>/saved</code> should be removed only after a deliberate data review.</p>`,sources:[["nix-flatpak","https://github.com/gmodena/nix-flatpak"],["Flatpak basic commands","https://docs.flatpak.org/en/latest/using-flatpak.html"]],quiz:["Why do theme extensions use persist = false?",["Their code is insecure","They have no per-app data to preserve","It disables their updates"],1,"Persistence controls saved application data, not update behavior or trust."]},
{id:"sandbox",group:"DAILY WORK",title:"Understand app permissions",subtitle:"A sandbox is a set of capabilities, not a single security switch.",lab:"sandbox",body:`
<p>Flatpak separates an application from much of the host, then grants capabilities. Filesystems expose paths; sockets expose display/audio interfaces; devices expose hardware; shared namespaces permit networking or IPC; D-Bus policy permits named services. Every grant should match a task the app needs.</p>
<p><code>xdg-download:ro</code> gives read-only Downloads access. Without <code>:ro</code>, a filesystem grant normally permits writing. <code>dri</code> exposes graphics devices. <code>all</code> is much broader and is retained for specific apps requiring direct device access. An XWayland exception trades isolation for compatibility.</p>
<h2>Two different filesystem policies</h2><p>Denying <code>host</code> and <code>home</code> removes those broad grants. It does not remove narrower inherited permissions such as Documents. A filesystem reset removes inherited path grants before rebuilding the intended allowlist. Per-app policy needs testing; some applications use portals well, while others expect direct folders.</p>
<p>A <strong>portal</strong> lets the desktop mediate a request, such as selecting a file or sharing a screen. Granting a directory permanently is different from selecting a file through a portal. Previously granted portal access can outlive a single dialog. A network permission is usually all-or-nothing, not a domain allowlist.</p>
<details><summary>Why not block everything globally?</summary><p>Apps can fail before showing a helpful error: missing GPU access, incompatible Wayland support, camera access, or document paths. Reduce capabilities deliberately, test the actual task, and keep an explicit exception when needed. Isolation must remain understandable.</p></details>
<details><summary>What about native apps and AppImages?</summary><p>Native apps do not become sandboxed merely because Nix installs them. AppImage compatibility also does not itself confine an app. They run with their process’s normal user permissions unless another sandbox or enforced policy applies.</p></details>`,sources:[["Flatpak sandbox permissions","https://docs.flatpak.org/en/latest/sandbox-permissions.html"],["Flatpak command reference","https://docs.flatpak.org/en/latest/flatpak-command-reference.html"]],quiz:["Does !home remove every Documents grant inherited from an app?",["Yes","No; narrower filesystem grants can remain","Only for native apps"],1,"Broad path denial and a reset of inherited filesystem grants are different policies."]},
{id:"persistence",group:"STATE & IDENTITY",title:"What survives a reboot",subtitle:"A temporary root does not make your retained data temporary.",lab:"persistence",body:`
<p>Shadow’s root is a 4 GiB tmpfs. Its contents disappear on reboot unless a path is backed by persistent storage. LUKS protects a Btrfs filesystem whose <code>/nix</code>, <code>/saved</code>, and swap subvolumes survive.</p>
<pre><code>NVMe → LUKS2 → Btrfs
                  ├─ /nix    package store
                  ├─ /saved  selected system/user data + repository
                  └─ /swap   encrypted disk swap
RAM → /            temporary root</code></pre>
<p>Preservation exposes selected backing directories/files at the paths applications expect. Declaring a path describes that relationship; it is not a backup. The authoritative declaration is <code>modules/nixos/storage/preservation.nix</code>. Direct paths already under <code>/saved</code>, such as the repository and deployment identity, do not need another preservation mount.</p>
<h2>Important choices</h2><ul><li>Documents, Projects, Books, media, keyring state, browser state, editor data, and selected desktop state are retained.</li><li>Downloads is intentionally temporary. Move valuable downloads into a retained directory.</li><li>Flatpak directories derive from the catalog. Theme extensions are excluded.</li><li>KeePassXC settings and the Rust Book browser profile are now retained with private directory modes.</li><li>Runtime decrypted files under <code>/run/secrets</code> remain ephemeral.</li><li>Logs retain up to seven days / 128 MiB. Core dumps are disabled.</li></ul>
<p>Removing a preservation entry does not erase its backing directory. Conversely, deleting the backing directory destroys data independently of a configuration rollback. New preservation mounts also need migration care: data created in an unpreserved home path before activation should be backed up and checked before a mount hides or replaces that view.</p>
<details><summary>Why root:root 0700 or aqua:users 0700?</summary><p>Ownership selects who controls a file. Directory mode 0700 permits only its owner to traverse it. File mode 0600 permits owner read/write; 0400 is owner read-only. Mount parents need correct ownership too. Permissions reduce access but do not protect against root compromise.</p></details>`,sources:[["Preservation module","https://github.com/nix-community/preservation"],["systemd tmpfiles","https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd-tmpfiles.html"]],quiz:["You save a valuable file in Downloads, then reboot. What should you expect?",["It is always backed up","It may be lost; Downloads is not preserved","Nix copies it into the store"],1,"Move valuable files to Documents or another retained directory before reboot."]},
{id:"keys",group:"STATE & IDENTITY",title:"Every key has a different job",subtitle:"Stop treating disk, age, VPN, SSH, and signing keys as the same thing.",lab:"keys",body:`
<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Identity</th><th>Purpose</th><th>What is public?</th></tr><tr><td>LUKS credential</td><td>Unlock encrypted disk storage</td><td>Disk-format metadata, not the unlock secret</td></tr><tr><td>age identity</td><td>Decrypt SOPS-encrypted material</td><td>Recipient starting age1…</td></tr><tr><td>WireGuard key</td><td>Authenticate a tunnel peer</td><td>The corresponding VPN public key</td></tr><tr><td>SSH key</td><td>Authenticate SSH user or host</td><td>The public SSH key</td></tr><tr><td>Cache signing key</td><td>Verify substituted Nix results</td><td>The cache verification key</td></tr><tr><td>Secure Boot key</td><td>Establish trusted boot signatures</td><td>Enrolled verification certificates</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Your age setup has two independent private identities. Aqua’s editing identity is under <code>~/.config/sops/age/keys.txt</code>, backed by retained home state. Shadow’s deployment identity is <code>/saved/var/lib/sops-nix/key.txt</code>, protected for root. Their public recipients are in <code>.sops.yaml</code>.</p>
<p>SOPS encrypts each file’s data with a data key and wraps access for the configured recipients. In your setup either private identity can decrypt. Having two recipients is recovery redundancy, not a requirement to provide both keys together.</p>
<div class="banner">Adding a VPN or token normally reuses the existing age recipients. The VPN may bring its own WireGuard key; that does not require a new age identity.</div>
<p>A public recipient is safe to commit, but authorizing it is a policy decision: it names an identity that may decrypt. Never paste an age private identity into chat, a shell argument, a Nix string, or Git. Back up at least the editing identity with independent encryption off the laptop. Encrypting its only backup to itself makes recovery circular.</p>
<details><summary>When do age identities change?</summary><p>When an editing or machine identity is replaced, compromised, or newly authorized. Change the recipients, rewrap affected ciphertext with SOPS updatekeys, verify deployment, and rotate compromised provider credentials when necessary. An old private key may still decrypt old Git revisions.</p></details>`,sources:[["age project and design","https://github.com/FiloSottile/age"],["SOPS documentation","https://getsops.io/docs/"]],quiz:["You add another VPN provider. Do you normally need a fresh age identity?",["Yes, one for every app","No, encrypt its configuration to existing recipients","No, put its private key in flake.nix"],1,"age protects stored credentials; WireGuard authenticates the tunnel. Their identities have different jobs."]},
{id:"secrets",group:"STATE & IDENTITY",title:"Add, deploy & rotate a secret",subtitle:"Ciphertext in Git. Plaintext only where its consumer needs it.",lab:"secret",body:`
<p><strong>age</strong> supplies identities. <strong>SOPS</strong> encrypts and edits versionable secret files. <strong>sops-nix</strong> decrypts declared secrets during activation, assigns ownership/modes, and exposes runtime paths. An encrypted archive without a consumer is not an active service configuration.</p>
<h2>Your deployed secrets</h2><p>The user password hash is decrypted early using <code>neededForUsers</code>. Nix configuration is decrypted for Aqua and linked into the home configuration. The Wallhaven token is inserted into a runtime Noctalia template. GitHub and WireGuard ciphertext were archived separately; The networking stage exposes the archived VPN as a protected runtime file for native NetworkManager import.</p>
<h2>Your new everyday helper</h2><pre><code>secret list
secret status example
secret add example /private/path/example
secret add-token example
secret edit example</code></pre><p>add refuses an existing target and a plaintext source inside this repository. add-token uses a hidden local prompt. New ciphertext is published atomically without overwriting another writer’s file. list and status do not reveal values; edit deliberately opens plaintext in your local editor. Adding ciphertext still requires a consumer.</p><h2>Add a file-shaped secret</h2><pre><code>cd /saved/nixos-config
sops encrypt --filename-override encrypted-secrets/example.enc \
  --input-type binary --output-type binary \
  --output encrypted-secrets/example.enc /private/path/example</code></pre>
<p>Keep the plaintext source outside the repository. Then declare the consumer:</p><pre><code>sops.secrets.example = {
  sopsFile = ../../../encrypted-secrets/example.enc;
  format = "binary";
  owner = "service-user";
  mode = "0400";
};
# Use config.sops.secrets.example.path in a file-valued service option.</code></pre>
<p>The owner and path depend on the actual service. Check that the option reads a file at runtime. Do not use <code>builtins.readFile</code> on plaintext or interpolate an actual credential into a Nix setting: evaluated strings may enter the world-readable store.</p>
<h2>Why templates are safe here</h2><p>The tracked Noctalia TOML contains a token marker. Evaluation replaces it with an opaque sops-nix placeholder, then activation inserts the plaintext into a protected runtime file. Edits to that generated file are temporary; durable edits belong to the tracked template. A token can still be copied by its consuming app, so runtime host security remains important.</p>
<h2>Rotation and recovery</h2><p>If a provider token leaked, revoke/rotate it at the provider. Re-encryption cannot revoke it. If one age identity is lost, the other can rewrap secrets for a replacement. If both are lost and no independent backup exists, public recipients cannot reconstruct the keys. A KeePass database is already encrypted and mutable; back it up directly rather than wrapping every change in SOPS.</p>
<details><summary>Changing your login password</summary><p>Users are declarative. A passwd change is not authoritative across activations. Generate a new password hash through a hidden local prompt, encrypt it into the password-hash ciphertext, build and test while keeping an authenticated recovery terminal, then verify fresh login and sudo before switching. Also consider whether the login keyring password needs a coordinated update.</p></details>`,sources:[["sops-nix examples and templates","https://github.com/Mic92/sops-nix"],["SOPS CLI and key rotation","https://getsops.io/docs/"]],quiz:["Does encrypting a leaked token make the leaked copy invalid?",["Yes","No; rotate or revoke it at the provider","Only after reboot"],1,"Encryption protects stored copies. Provider-side rotation invalidates exposed credentials."]},
{id:"network",group:"NETWORK & PROTECTION",title:"DNS, VPN & the firewall",subtitle:"Different layers protect different parts of a connection.",lab:"network",body:`
<p><strong>DNS</strong> translates names to addresses. <strong>DNSCrypt/DoH</strong> encrypts queries to a resolver. <strong>DNSSEC</strong> provides validation-related assurances about DNS data, not secrecy. <strong>HTTPS</strong> protects an application connection. A <strong>VPN</strong> tunnels routed traffic to another network. A <strong>firewall</strong> allows or rejects traffic according to rules.</p>
<p>Encrypted DNS is not anonymity: the resolver still sees queries, and connection destinations can reveal activity. A VPN moves trust to the VPN operator; it does not make logged-in web accounts anonymous. Temporary IPv6 addresses and randomized Wi-Fi MACs reduce some stable network identifiers but do not replace routing or app privacy.</p>
<h2>Shadow’s ownership</h2><p>NetworkManager manages connections. <code>dns = "none"</code> keeps it from replacing the DNS policy. DNSCrypt provides the system resolver. Browser-internal encrypted DNS can bypass that system path, so inspect browser settings when testing. Split-DNS corporate VPNs need deliberate integration rather than assuming a fixed public resolver fits them.</p>
<p>The revised configuration listens on both 127.0.0.1 and ::1. Cloudflare and Scaleway remain the deliberately selected resolvers: their catalog stamps advertise DNSSEC, no logging, and no filtering. The require_* settings apply only if automatic selection replaces server_names, so they are not presented as enforcement on those named providers. Resolver bootstrap and endpoint resolution still need network access before the tunnel can connect. Captive portals and filtered networks can behave differently, so connection testing matters.</p>
<h2>Firewall direction matters</h2><p>Closing inbound ports does not block ordinary applications from making outbound connections. A service bound to <code>127.0.0.1</code> is local; <code>0.0.0.0</code> means all IPv4 interfaces. A broad bind plus a firewall is a different design from a loopback-only service.</p>
<p>Kavita is now configured for loopback only and starts on demand. Its Books mount is read-only, state remains writable, and its old Wi-Fi firewall opening is removed. Open http://127.0.0.1:8083 after starting it with book_library on. Sharing it later requires a deliberate bind/firewall change. A development server’s convenience flag <code>--host 0.0.0.0</code> should be intentional. IPv6 must be considered separately from IPv4.</p>`,sources:[["DNSCrypt configuration reference","https://github.com/DNSCrypt/dnscrypt-proxy/blob/master/dnscrypt-proxy/example-dnscrypt-proxy.toml"],["NetworkManager reference","https://networkmanager.dev/docs/api/latest/"],["WireGuard overview","https://www.wireguard.com/"]],quiz:["Does an inbound firewall stop every app from contacting the internet?",["Yes","No; outbound policy is a different concern","Only when a VPN is active"],1,"The configured inbound firewall controls incoming reachability, not a per-app outbound domain policy."]},
{id:"vpn",group:"NETWORK & PROTECTION",title:"Add & operate a VPN",subtitle:"Connect encrypted credentials to a real connection controller.",body:`
<p>The desktop controller remains NetworkManager. A WireGuard configuration includes a local private key, address, peer public key, endpoint, and allowed IP ranges. Allowed IPs help select which peer handles which destinations; routing rules decide how traffic reaches the interface. These are not equivalent to a kill switch.</p>
<h2>Use the native NetworkManager workflow</h2><pre><code>nmcli connection show
# Only if this profile is not already present:
sudo nmcli connection import type wireguard file /run/secrets/Shadow-BG-20.conf
nmcli connection modify id Shadow-BG-20 connection.autoconnect no
nmcli connection up id Shadow-BG-20
nmcli connection down id Shadow-BG-20</code></pre><p>The existing connection is already managed by NetworkManager. Do not import it again merely because you rebuilt. The only new declaration decrypts the existing archive into a root-only runtime file. There is no custom VPN command, Python converter, boot-time import service, or second connection controller. Use the desktop network controls for everyday connect/disconnect.</p><h2>The safe addition workflow</h2><ol><li>Obtain the provider configuration through its trusted channel; keep it outside Git.</li><li>Encrypt it using SOPS and the existing recipients.</li><li>Declare a SOPS runtime file and import it through NetworkManager only if a new profile is needed.</li><li>Leave autoconnect off until the profile is verified.</li><li>Connect through the desktop network controls or nmcli.</li><li>Verify IPv4, IPv6, DNS, endpoint reachability, and intended LAN access.</li></ol>
<p><code>modules/nixos/security/secrets.nix</code> declares the encrypted archive’s runtime path. To add a second VPN, encrypt its provider file, add an equivalent secret declaration with a unique .conf path, and import it through NetworkManager. The protected NetworkManager connection directory is already preserved across reboot. Importing is an explicit operation; rebuilding does not alter the currently connected VPN. Imported profiles need their autoconnect preference reviewed before relying on their behavior.</p>
<h2>Full tunnel, split tunnel, and fail-closed</h2><p>A full tunnel aims to route general internet traffic through the VPN. A split tunnel covers selected destinations. A fail-closed policy blocks unwanted traffic when the tunnel is absent; it must retain exceptions for VPN endpoint access, necessary DNS bootstrap, and whatever local networking you choose. It cannot be safely inferred just from the existence of a WireGuard interface.</p>
<p>With the present system resolver ownership, do not assume the provider’s DNS field automatically becomes the system resolver. It may be ignored by the existing policy. Before claiming privacy, test DNS routing and browser DNS, and check that IPv6 does not take an unintended path. The configuration does not claim a verified kill switch.</p>
<details><summary>What changes when you add a second VPN?</summary><p>Usually a new provider config/ciphertext and profile entry. Keep names unambiguous, avoid simultaneous competing default routes, choose autoconnect explicitly, and connect one at a time until you understand the routing. Reuse the existing age identities.</p></details>`,sources:[["WireGuard quick start","https://www.wireguard.com/quickstart/"],["NetworkManager WireGuard settings","https://networkmanager.dev/docs/api/latest/settings-wireguard.html"]],quiz:["What proves a tunnel is leak-free?",["The VPN icon is visible","Explicit routing/DNS/IPv6 and failure tests","The config is encrypted"],1,"Encryption at rest and an active interface do not establish the actual packet path."]},
{id:"security",group:"NETWORK & PROTECTION",title:"Security without magic labels",subtitle:"Understand what each protection covers—and what it cannot cover.",body:`
<p>LUKS protects retained data while the disk is locked. It does not block malware running as you after login. SOPS protects selected secrets in Git/store copies, but applications consuming them can read them. An ephemeral root removes undeclared root state after reboot, while preserved histories and app profiles remain.</p>
<p>USBGuard controls USB device authorization. Its built-in allowlist is essential: blindly blocking those devices can break the keyboard, camera, and Bluetooth. Runtime approval needs a persistent policy path if it should survive activation. Review a device before allowing it.</p>
<p>AppArmor is loaded, but useful protection requires enforced profiles that cover actual workloads. A native app without a profile is not magically confined. Service hardening can restrict writable paths, devices, namespaces, privileges, and address families. Add restrictions with the service’s data and workflow in mind.</p>
<h2>Authentication and session details</h2><p>Root password login is locked; Aqua authenticates with a SOPS-deployed hash and has administrative access through sudo-rs. The greetd login stack unlocks the GNOME keyring. A documented workaround bypasses the nested user-manager PAM call after a deadlock; it must be investigated through actual session tests before removing it.</p>
<p>Niri/Noctalia supplies idle locking and suspend behavior. Locking before suspend must be tested interactively. An unlocked keyring can serve credentials to authorized apps; secret-service access should be granted intentionally.</p>
<h2>Boot trust is a separate layer</h2><p>Secure Boot and TPM auto-unlock are not configured by these stages. Secure Boot signs/verifies boot artifacts; it does not replace disk encryption. TPM unlock links access to measurements/policy and needs a recovery credential. NVIDIA and OpenRazer complicate module-signing/lockdown decisions. Enrollment belongs to a prepared recovery exercise, not a quick cleanup.</p>
<p>The previously exposed Wallhaven token still requires provider-side rotation if that has not already happened. A clean current-source scan cannot prove it was revoked or that old Git history contains no secrets.</p>`,sources:[["AppArmor project","https://apparmor.net/"],["systemd service sandboxing","https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html"],["Lanzaboote recovery considerations","https://nix-community.github.io/lanzaboote/explanation/troubleshooting.html"]],quiz:["Does AppArmor enablement prove all apps are confined?",["Yes","No; enforced profiles and covered processes matter","Only for Flatpak"],1,"The security module must have applicable enforced policies; enablement alone is not coverage."]},
{id:"development",group:"OPERATIONS",title:"Project environments & editors",subtitle:"Your laptop supplies orchestration; each project supplies its toolchain.",body:`
<p>Home Manager provides shared tools such as Zed, Delta, devenv, direnv, nixd, nixfmt, statix, and deadnix. Rust, Node, Bun, package managers, formatters, and language servers should follow each project’s requirements in its devenv configuration.</p>
<pre><code>devenv-new my-project
# In an existing project with devenv.nix:
devenv-enable</code></pre><p>Your helpers initialize a new environment without replacing an existing one, or add the canonical .envrc if missing. Review .envrc before approval: entering a directory with an approved .envrc can execute its environment setup. Changing its contents requires new approval.</p>
<p>Commit the project’s environment source and locks. Generated .devenv/.direnv state stays out of Git. Retained direnv approvals and devenv state avoid repeating trust setup every reboot. Nix GC roots protect referenced environments.</p>
<h2>Editor ownership</h2><p>Zed’s configuration is divided into settings, languages, LSP definitions, keymaps, and extensions because those are substantial. Delta comes from the official flake; its user configuration/state remains retained. Both still operate with application and agent permissions independent of package reproducibility.</p>
<h2>Local AI costs</h2><p>The optional local-AI module is currently parked. It would expose a loopback llama-swap endpoint and load models on demand. CUDA compilation can be expensive when Nixpkgs changes; importing the module has build consequences even if the service does not run at boot. Model files belong in retained storage and need their own space/backup policy. Provider-hosted inference has different privacy and account costs.</p>
<div class="banner">When adapting another project’s setup, follow the target’s actual lockfile. A Bun project should not inherit pnpm assumptions just because a reference environment uses pnpm.</div>`,sources:[["devenv getting started","https://devenv.sh/getting-started/"],["direnv documentation","https://direnv.net/"],["Delta’s Nix packaging","https://github.com/zed-industries/delta-nix"]],quiz:["Why preserve direnv approvals?",["To approve future changes automatically","To retain approval of unchanged reviewed .envrc content","To disable its trust check"],1,"Approval is content-based. Changed files still require review and approval."]},
{id:"recovery",group:"OPERATIONS",title:"Recovery, backups & cleanup",subtitle:"Configurations roll back. Data needs another copy.",lab:"recovery",body:`
<p>A generation references a particular system closure. Booting an older generation can recover from a broken driver/configuration. A Btrfs snapshot captures local filesystem state at a point in time. An external backup protects against loss of that disk. These mechanisms have different failure boundaries.</p>
<p>These stages do not add external backup jobs or snapshots. Your next recovery priority is a tested encrypted off-device backup of documents, projects, password databases, age recovery identity, and selected app state. Recovery keys cannot exist only on the failed laptop.</p>
<h2>Cleanup on Shadow</h2><p>Friday’s nh cleanup retains recent generations and leaves explicit GC roots intact using <code>--no-gcroots</code>. Nix collects unreachable store paths. Store optimization hard-links identical files afterward. Do not manually delete random store directories or the deduplication index.</p>
<p>Monthly Btrfs scrub verifies checksums across the shared filesystem. A single-device scrub can detect damage but usually cannot repair it without another good copy. SMART gives health indicators, not a guarantee against sudden failure. zRAM compresses memory pages; encrypted disk swap is available as overflow.</p>
<h2>If something breaks</h2><ol><li>Keep the authenticated terminal open and record the symptom.</li><li>If an activation change broke a service, restore a known generation or reboot the previous default after a temporary test.</li><li>If a boot change failed, select an older generation from the boot menu.</li><li>If user data is damaged, use a data backup; switching generations cannot reconstruct it.</li><li>If a key is lost, use the independent recovery identity/backup and rewrap secrets.</li></ol>
<details><summary>Reinstallation is different from rebuilding</summary><p>Disko destroy/format operations erase storage. Do not run them to validate a module. Reinstallation must verify the target disk, restore age identities before deploying encrypted users, and preserve a usable rescue path. The disk layout’s name is not authorization to format it.</p></details>`,sources:[["Btrfs scrub","https://btrfs.readthedocs.io/en/latest/btrfs-scrub.html"],["Nix garbage collection","https://nix.dev/manual/nix/latest/package-management/garbage-collection"],["Restic backup documentation","https://restic.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"]],quiz:["Your password database is accidentally deleted. Which mechanism can restore its contents?",["A verified data backup","Changing flake.lock","A package-generation rollback alone"],0,"A configuration generation does not contain your mutable password database."]},
{id:"debug",group:"OPERATIONS",title:"Find the cause of a failure",subtitle:"Separate evaluation, build, activation, and runtime errors.",body:`
<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Typical symptom</th><th>First check</th></tr><tr><td>Evaluation</td><td>Unknown option, type error, missing path</td><td>Imports, option declaration, staged new files</td></tr><tr><td>Build</td><td>Download/hash/compiler failure</td><td>Specific derivation log, network/cache access</td></tr><tr><td>Activation</td><td>Managed-file collision, permission/mount error</td><td>Activation output and relevant ownership</td></tr><tr><td>Runtime</td><td>App crashes, no DNS, unavailable keyring</td><td>Service/app logs and actual session behavior</td></tr></table></div>
<pre><code>systemctl --failed
systemctl --user --failed
journalctl -b -u dnscrypt-proxy
systemctl status kavita
ss -lntu
niri validate --config ~/.config/niri/config.kdl</code></pre>
<p>User services and the system service manager have different scopes. A graphical-session keyring problem should be tested in that graphical session. A sandboxed tool failing to contact D-Bus or the Nix cache is an execution-environment limitation, not proof the system service is broken.</p>
<p>If the same app symptom appears from both Flatpak and a native package, investigate shared layers such as display, D-Bus, keyring, GPU, and user state. Do not repeatedly replace packages without evidence. Element’s Secret Service integration under Niri is an example of a shared session concern.</p>
<h2>Inspect without exposing secrets</h2><p>Use metadata: service state, paths, ownership, modes, public recipient count, encryption status. Avoid printing private identities, NetworkManager secret fields, environment files, or token-bearing command lines. If a process is listening broadly, its name/working directory is often enough to identify its owner.</p>
<h2>Read the final configuration</h2><pre><code>nix eval --json /saved/nixos-config#nixosConfigurations.shadow.config.networking.nameservers
nix eval /saved/nixos-config#nixosConfigurations.shadow.config.security.apparmor.enable</code></pre><p>Change one cause at a time and verify the affected behavior. Repeatedly running unrelated tests adds noise without strengthening evidence.</p>`,sources:[["NixOS troubleshooting reference","https://nixos.org/manual/nixos/stable/"],["systemctl manual","https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemctl.html"]],quiz:["A tool cannot open the user D-Bus socket. What does that prove?",["The keyring is absent","Only that this tool could not reach that socket","The VPN is disconnected"],1,"Test from the relevant session before concluding a service is absent."]},
{id:"inventory",group:"REFERENCE",title:"The app & file map",subtitle:"Searchable entry points into the actual repository.",lab:"inventory",body:`
<p>Use the app catalog below to locate a Flatpak entry. Origin is the configured remote; runtime installations can differ until reconciliation succeeds. Exceptions describe declared overrides, not a complete effective-permissions audit.</p>
<h2>Native and special application owners</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Application</th><th>Source owner</th></tr><tr><td>Delta</td><td>Official flake + <code>modules/home/programs/delta.nix</code></td></tr><tr><td>Brave</td><td><code>modules/home/programs/brave.nix</code></td></tr><tr><td>Zed</td><td><code>modules/home/programs/zed/</code></td></tr><tr><td>KeePassXC</td><td><code>modules/home/programs/keepassxc.nix</code></td></tr><tr><td>Obsidian, Anki, Mullvad Browser</td><td><code>modules/home/packages/apps.nix</code></td></tr><tr><td>GNOME file/system apps</td><td><code>modules/nixos/desktop/apps.nix</code></td></tr><tr><td>Steam and game tools</td><td><code>modules/nixos/gaming/steam.nix</code></td></tr><tr><td>Nahhas Cinema local AppImage</td><td><code>modules/home/desktop/local-apps.nix</code></td></tr></table></div>
<p>Binary updates for the local AppImage are still outside the flake graph. Keep its provenance and update method clear. No extra launcher is needed for Delta because the official package supplies one.</p>`,sources:[["Repository package collection","https://search.nixos.org/packages"],["Flatpak reference","https://docs.flatpak.org/en/latest/"]]},
{id:"sources",group:"REFERENCE",title:"Go deeper & test yourself",subtitle:"Primary sources, an ordered learning path, and questions you should be able to explain.",body:`
<h2>A useful order</h2><ol><li>Read Nix expressions and evaluate small examples in <code>nix repl</code>.</li><li>Understand module imports, types, merging, and priorities.</li><li>Trace Shadow’s host and user graph.</li><li>Practice an app addition: owner, permissions, persistence, evaluation.</li><li>Trace a secret from ciphertext to runtime consumer.</li><li>Explain disk encryption, generation rollback, and backup boundaries.</li><li>Inspect DNS and VPN routing before changing failure policy.</li></ol>
<h2>Explain these without looking</h2><ul><li>Why is an age recipient safe in Git, but its identity is not?</li><li>Why does a new VPN not normally need a new age identity?</li><li>Why can a flake update leave the running system unchanged?</li><li>Why can a generation rollback fail to repair app data?</li><li>Why is a closed inbound firewall not an outbound app policy?</li><li>Why might a Flatpak still access Documents after !home?</li><li>Why does a file under /run disappear while an app profile survives?</li><li>Why does a Nix package need runtime launch testing after it builds?</li></ul>
<p>This guide is a starting point for mastery, not a guarantee of complete knowledge. Real confidence comes from small changes, predicting their effects, testing those predictions, and learning to recover. The linked sources let you move from the practical model to exact semantics.</p>
<details><summary>Further topics once these foundations are comfortable</summary><p>Package derivations and overlays; fixed-output derivations; store closures and GC roots; systemd credentials and sandboxing; nftables routing and firewall hooks; WireGuard policy routing; UEFI trust and measured boot; Btrfs subvolumes and backup consistency; agent/application runtime permissions.</p></details>`,sources:[["Nix language","https://nix.dev/tutorials/nix-language"],["Module system deep dive","https://nix.dev/tutorials/module-system/deep-dive"],["Nixpkgs manual","https://nixos.org/manual/nixpkgs/stable/"],["NixOS manual","https://nixos.org/manual/nixos/stable/"],["Home Manager manual","https://nix-community.github.io/home-manager/"],["Preservation","https://github.com/nix-community/preservation"],["SOPS","https://getsops.io/docs/"],["age","https://github.com/FiloSottile/age"],["sops-nix","https://github.com/Mic92/sops-nix"],["Flatpak sandboxing","https://docs.flatpak.org/en/latest/sandbox-permissions.html"],["nix-flatpak","https://github.com/gmodena/nix-flatpak"],["NetworkManager WireGuard","https://networkmanager.dev/docs/api/latest/settings-wireguard.html"],["DNSCrypt configuration","https://github.com/DNSCrypt/dnscrypt-proxy/blob/master/dnscrypt-proxy/example-dnscrypt-proxy.toml"],["systemd execution and sandboxing","https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html"],["devenv","https://devenv.sh/"],["Btrfs documentation","https://btrfs.readthedocs.io/"],["Official Delta flake","https://github.com/zed-industries/delta-nix"]]}
];
